WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
The Vision Transformer.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.900
It skips convolution entirely, and it is the vision half of every multimodal LLM.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.333
ViT asks a heretical question.

4
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:15.233
What if we throw away convolution and treat an image like text?

5
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:19.467
Each small patch of the image becomes a word, or rather, a token.

6
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:22.200
Here is the whole pipeline.

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.133
Start with a two hundred twenty four pixel image.

8
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:29.633
Chop it into one hundred ninety six patches, each sixteen by sixteen.

9
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:33.488
Flatten each patch and project it through one linear layer.

10
00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:34.633
Now we have tokens.

11
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:40.333
Add position embeddings for patch order, and a learnable CLS token to collect the summary.

12
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:43.133
Feed it all to a standard transformer encoder.

13
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:46.881
And the classification head reads the CLS output.

14
00:00:46.881 --> 00:00:47.233
Done.

15
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:49.500
There's a tradeoff.

16
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.904
A CNN has locality built in.

17
00:00:51.904 --> 00:00:54.233
ViT must learn it from scratch.

18
00:00:54.533 --> 00:01:01.100
On ImageNet alone it loses to ResNet, but pretrained on hundreds of millions of images, it wins.

19
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.700
Self attention relates any patch to any other in one hop.

20
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.800
Data hungry, but it scales.

21
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.200
Here it is in code.

22
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.700
We load the image processor and the pretrained ViT.

23
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.933
The processor resizes the picture to two hundred twenty four square.

24
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:23.400
And the patch embeddings come out as one hundred ninety six tokens, each seven hundred sixty eight numbers long.

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.833
Why does the patch grid matter in practice?

26
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:31.033
Ask a multimodal model whether a storm frame shows an eye wall.

27
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:36.433
It literally sees patch tokens, so a ten pixel feature can vanish inside one patch.

28
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:41.148
So you tile and zoom the input, and you never ask for pixel coordinates.

29
00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:43.600
The patch grid is your debugging manual.

30
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.612
Let's recap.

31
00:01:45.612 --> 00:01:49.133
An image becomes patches, and patches become tokens.

32
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:53.633
Add position embeddings and a CLS token, then a plain transformer.

33
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:57.033
It is data hungry, but wins with big pretraining.

34
00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:00.867
And it is the vision half of multimodal LLMs.

35
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:01.967
Next up: YOLO.
